As Jiqiang points out, this is more of a stats question than
a Stan question.  I didn't understand the R notation.

Might it make sense in this case to define three boolean variables for smoking,
drinking and exercising, say y[1], y[2] and y[3] and then model y jointly in
a multivariate regression?   The simplest thing to do would be the logistic
version of:

    http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seemingly_unrelated_regressions

where you have separate regressions for the expectation of each variable, but
the errors are tied in a multivariate structure to allow correlations.

I don't actually know if anybody uses this.  Is there a section in Andrew
and Jennifer's book on multiple output regression like this?  (I don't recall
one.)

- Bob

On 8/4/13 10:10 PM, Jiqiang Guo wrote:
> I do not really understand the question here.  But as we have always put, what Stan does is to specify the log of the
> posterior density function of continuous parameters and use NUTS (a variant of HMC) to draw samples from the posterior.
>   However, how to specify the posterior and make sure the correctness are totally up to the user.  So if you can express
> your idea of combing information into a posterior distribution, Stan can help.  Then the real question is whether you
> can write down the posterior mathematically.
>
> --
> Jiqiang
>

> On Sun, Aug 4, 2013 at 7:46 AM, Marcel Jonker <mfjo...@hotmail.com <mailto:mfjo...@hotmail.com>> wrote:
>
>     Dear STAN-users,
>
>     Suppose I have 3 dummy variables, indicating whether a survey respondent living in area i smokes, drinks, and
>     exercises. To estimate the (within-area) joint distribution of these variables, I model the survey data as:
>
>     n[i,1:K] ~ dmulti( phi[i,1:K] , N[i] )
>     phi[i,1:K] ~ dirichlet( [1,..,1] )   or:   phi[i,1:K] ~ logisticNormal( [mu[],sigma[] )
>
>     where 1:K represents the 2^3 combinations of the dummy variables. Because the survey data is relatively sparse, I
>     want to combine the estimation of phi with additional information about the marginal distribution of each risk
>     factor. Suppose I can model the marginal distribution of each risk factor as:
>
>     logit( prob_smoking[i] ) <- inprod( b1[],X1[i,] )
>     logit( prob_drinking[i] ) <- inprod( b2[],X2[i,] )
>     logit( prob_exercising[i] ) <- inprod( b3[],X3[i,] ) ,
>
>     does it make sense (and is it possible?) to combine the two sources of information, e.g. by specifying something
>     like this in STAN:
>
>     sum( phi[i,1:K] .* [0 0 0 0 1 1 1 1] ) <- inv_logit( b1*X1) )
>     sum( phi[i,1:K] .* [0 0 1 1 0 0 1 1] ) <- inv_logit( b2*X2) )
>     sum( phi[i,1:K] .* [0 1 0 1 0 1 0 1] ) <- inv_logit( b3*X3) )
>
>     where the sum of the elements of phi is equal to the prob_smoking, prob_drinking, and prob_exercising?
>
>     Thanks!
>     Marcel
>
