Yes, the number of rows of A is usually much less than the number of columns, giving the solution space (ncol(A)-nrow(A)) dimensions. A basic case would be the unit simplex, where A = [1,1,1] and b = 1 (and if x = [x,y,z] this is equivalent to the equation x+y+z = 1). There are ways to sample from that space that do not require a random walk (x = rexp(3); x = x/sum(x)), however this changes when you add rows of A, which is equivalent to slicing the solution space by a plane.

